WEBVTT

00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:14,620
why are some cancers fast growing and some cancers

00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:16,660
slow growing?

00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:23,300
So the speed of replication is the speed

00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,660
of the growth of the cancer.

00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:30,620
And why is that?

00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,940
Good question.

00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:35,300
There's multiple different metabolic factors

00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:38,820
that are taking place with a cancer.

00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:43,140
And it really has to do with how whatever

00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:45,780
is causing the cancer, whatever caused the cancer,

00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:51,460
whatever damage the DNA in the first place,

00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:55,100
what rate that replication is taking place.

00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:58,740
And that can change through a person's cancer.

00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:00,660
It does change constantly.

00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:02,700
Because as you go through some treatment,

00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,780
it slows the cancer growth.

00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,660
And then the cancer can take off again.

00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:08,340
And then you do something different.

00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:11,900
It can slow the cancer growth so that it can take off again.

00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,500
So that rate of replication changes.

00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:19,580
So there's just multiple factors that affect

00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:26,260
the close tie-in rate of apoptosis at the same time.

00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,780
Because if you could be killing the cancer

00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,220
that faster than it's replicating,

00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:34,500
then you're going to be winning your battle.

